## Lavalier or Headset: Which One Actually Fits Your Event

Both are hands free, and both connect to the same wireless receiver chain our technician runs, but they solve different problems, which is exactly what a general search for microphone rental tends to skip.

A lavalier clips to clothing, a jacket lapel, a dress collar, a lanyard, and stays out of sight, which matters for photography and video at a wedding or a filmed keynote. The tradeoff is placement: it sits a hand's width or more from the mouth, fixed wherever it is clipped, so clothing movement, a scarf, or a turned head can shift how consistent the pickup is, and it is more prone to feedback at higher PA volumes than a mic held or worn closer to the source.

The AUDIX HT5 headband sits at a fixed, close distance from the mouth regardless of how much the wearer turns their head or moves across a stage, which gives it a more consistent level for a presenter who paces, gestures widely, or turns to address a screen behind them. The tradeoff is visibility: a headband mic is not discreet, it reads clearly in photos and on camera, which rules it out for a wedding ceremony where a couple does not want visible hardware in their photos.

Neither is objectively better. A wedding officiant who mostly stands still and wants nothing visible in the ceremony photos is a lavalier candidate. A corporate presenter who walks the stage and gestures through a slide deck is closer to what the headband was built for. Tell us which one fits your event when you book, rather than assume a default, and we will confirm availability.

## Where This Gets Used: Officiants, Presenters and Panels

**A wedding officiant who needs both hands free.** Our [unique wedding ceremony rentals](/blog/unique-wedding-ceremony-rentals/) and [essential items for wedding rentals](/blog/essential-items-for-wedding-rentals/) guides are both explicit that the [Wedding Pack's](/packages/wedding/) included microphone is a wireless handheld, and that a lavalier is a separate add on, not a free swap. This page is where that add on gets its full detail: an officiant who reads from a book and gestures during the vows is the clearest lavalier use case in our own catalog.

**A corporate presenter who moves and gestures on stage.** The [MICE Pack](/packages/mice/) and [Basic MICE Pack](/packages/basic-mice/) both put a technician on site managing a gooseneck and a wireless handheld mic as standard. A presenter who wants to walk away from the podium, point at a screen, or run a live demo with both hands is exactly the scenario a lavalier or a headband mic gets added for, on top of the package's standard microphone. See our [AV technician hire](/blog/av-technician-hire/) guide for how that technician manages microphone handoffs during a live event.

**A panel or Q&A format with multiple speakers.** Passing a single wireless handheld mic between three or four panelists is slower and noisier than it needs to be. With two complete lavalier sets, two panelists can each wear one for the duration of a session, while the moderator or additional speakers still use the package's included wireless handheld for audience questions. This is a real limit, not a marketing claim: two simultaneous lavalier channels, not more, is what we can confirm today.

We do not serve a fitness class or dance instructor market with this equipment. It has never come up in a real booking on our side, so we are not going to invent that use case here.

